Understanding NGINX Ingress Controller on AWS — Why We Use It & How It Works

When deploying applications on EKS cluster, one common challenge is:
👉 How do we expose our services to the outside world — in a scalable, manageable, and secure way?

That’s where the NGINX Ingress Controller comes in. Let’s understand why, how, and when to use it.

🧠 Why Use NGINX Ingress Controller on AWS?
While AWS offers native load balancers (like ALB and NLB), they don’t natively understand Kubernetes Ingress resources.

To fill this gap, we use the NGINX Ingress Controller to:
✅ Read and enforce Kubernetes Ingress rules
✅ Route traffic based on host/path
✅ Enable features like SSL termination, request rewrites, rate limiting, and authentication
✅ Provide a cloud-agnostic, open-source option that works across all environments

⚙️ What Happens When You Deploy It?
Bash >>> kubectl apply -f https://lnkd.in/dPja4-dx

This does the following:
>> Deploys the NGINX Ingress Controller Pod into your cluster
>> Creates a Service of type LoadBalancer for that pod
>> AWS provisions a native ELB or NLB, assigning a public IP or DNS name
>> You map your application’s domain (from the Ingress resource) to this DNS/IP

🌐 Traffic Flow Summary:
User hits https://yourdomain.com
 ↓
AWS Load Balancer (ELB/NLB)
 ↓
NGINX Ingress Controller Pod
 ↓
Ingress rules determine routing
 ↓
ClusterIP Service
 ↓
Application Pods

Even if your Kubernetes Service is of type NodePort, traffic will still reach your pods — because the Ingress Controller bridges the outside world with your internal services.

🚪 Other Options?
Yes — you could use the AWS ALB Ingress Controller. It's a great native alternative when:
>> You want tight integration with AWS services
>> You prefer AWS to manage the lifecycle of ALBs
>> You don't need advanced HTTP features NGINX offers
But if you value flexibility, cloud-agnostic setups, and custom routing logic, then NGINX is often the better choice.

🔚 Final Thoughts
Ingress Controllers are powerful tools that abstract the complexity of traffic routing in Kubernetes.
✅ Use NGINX when you want full control and customization
✅ Use AWS ALB Ingress for a fully managed AWS-native solution
Either way, understanding how traffic flows helps you design better, scalable architectures.
